        {
          "n": 2,
          "layout": "RDS-CAL-01",
          "suite": "phase 1",
          "mode": "open",
          "detail": "not executed",
          "status": "pending",
          "why": "Observe how the vendor stack searches for an operating point: what it varies, in what order, what it measures to decide, how long it takes, what it writes to persistent storage, and what it does with a board that will not converge.",
          "started_at": null,
          "finished_at": null,
          "cases": []
        },
        {
          "n": 3,
          "layout": "RDS-ENG-01",
          "suite": "phase 1",
          "mode": "open",
          "detail": "not executed",
          "status": "pending",
          "why": "Settle the engine-count question that every hit-rate denominator and every nonce-range partition depends on, and settle it in the right terms. RDS-LOG-03 already observed the vendor stack reporting 100 devices and 23,600 engines for one board \u2014 exactly 100 \u00d7 236. The exactness is the finding: it argues a **nominal** figure the firmware assumes rather than a count it discovered. So the open question is no longer \"236 or 512\" but: **Settled 2026-09-15, and not by this case.** The public integration guide states it outright: 236 hashing engine tiles per ASIC, 4 engines per tile, 944 engines total, with the \u00d74 carried inside its own throughput estimate. Our measured 23,600 across 100 devices is 100 \u00d7 236, so the vendor stack counts **tiles**. There was never a conflict between 236 and 944; I built an argument between a tile count and a core count that were printed side by side. The 512 figure that appeared in a requirements document is a **third** claim and is supported by no source either of us can find. It needs its own provenance rather than being treated as the alternative to 236.",
          "started_at": null,
          "finished_at": null,
          "cases": []
        },
        {
          "n": 4,
          "layout": "RDS-THR-01",
          "suite": "phase 1",
          "mode": "open",
          "detail": "4 runs",
          "status": "skipped",
          "why": "Observe the **closed loop**, not the endpoints: how fan duty tracks die temperature once the controller is actually running. Rewritten 2026-09-15. The first version asked for \"the fan curve at powered idle\" and would have measured the wrong thing, because the loop does not start until calibration completes, then waits about 180 seconds to settle and averages several samples before its first move. A run that ends before that sees a constant duty and concludes there is no feedback. One did, and it was reported as a finding before Ronald corrected it. See `docs/rds/fan-control-law.md`.",
